The error message indicates that the test is failing because it expects only one HTTP request to be made, but it finds two requests instead. This is likely because the `subscribeToSubmited` method and the `submited$.next()` call are causing multiple HTTP requests to be made within the loop.

To fix this, you need to ensure that only one HTTP request is made per iteration of the loop. One way to achieve this is to reset the state of the component or the HTTP testing controller before each iteration. Here is a modified version of your test:

```ts
fit("should handle all error codes correctly", () => {
  component.form.setValue({
    name: "name",
    email: "email@gmail.com",
    password: "password",
  });

  errorCodes.forEach((code) => {
    const mockError = { listErrorDto: { code } };
    const expectedMessage =
      environment.errorMessages[
        code as keyof typeof environment.errorMessages
      ] || "Unknown error";

    // Reset the state before each iteration
    httpTestingController.verify();
    component.loading = true;
    component.albums = null;

    // Ejecutar la suscripción y enviar evento
    component.subscribeToSubmited();
    component.submited$.next();

    // Interceptar la petición y devolver error
    const req = httpTestingController.expectOne("api/validateUser");
    expect(req.request.method).toBe("POST");
    req.flush(mockError, { status: 400, statusText: "Bad Request" });

    // Verificar resultados
    expect(component.loading).toBeFalse();
    expect(component.albums).toEqual(expectedMessage);
  });

  // Verify that there are no outstanding requests
  httpTestingController.verify();
});
```

In this modified version, `httpTestingController.verify()` is called before each iteration to ensure that there are no outstanding requests from previous iterations. Additionally, the component's state is reset before each iteration to ensure that it starts fresh.

This should resolve the issue of multiple HTTP requests being made and allow your test to pass.
